y the king)\n- Attacks the knight on g5\n- Prevents Black from castling kingside safely\n- Forces Black to respond to concrete threats rather than continuing development\n- Creates tactical complications where White has better coordination\n\n**Reason:** Q: d1-h5 is the most forcing move that exploits Black's lack of kingside castling and creates immediate tactical threats against f7 and the g5 knight. This aggressive approach capitalizes on White's development lag by creating concrete problems for Black to solve, rather than allowing them to complete development comfortably.\n\n**Answer:** Q: d1-h5"}
