----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, N: b1-a3, N: b1-c3, N: b1-d2, P: b2-b4, P: b2-b3, B: c1-d2, B: c1-e3, B: c1-f4, B: c1-g5, B: c1-h6, P: c2-c3, K: e1-e2, K: e1-f1, K: e1-g1, P: f2-f4, P: f2-f3, P: g2-g4, P: g2-g3, R: h1-g1, R: h1-f1, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Evaluation: Let me analyze the position. I'm White and Black has a significant material advantage - Black has Queen on d5, two Knights (c6, e4), two Bishops (c8, f8), and two Rooks (a8, h8). I appear to be missing my Queen and several pieces. This is a very difficult position.\n\nKey observations:\n1. Black's Queen on d5 is centralized and powerful\n2. Black's Knight on e4 is well-placed and attacking\n3. My King on e1 hasn't castled yet and is vulnerable in the center\n4. I need to prioritize King safety immediately\n5. The Black pieces are coordinating well with potential threats\n\nPromising moves to consider:\n1. **K: e1-g1** (Castling kingside) - Gets my King to safety, activates the h1 Rook\n2. **B: c1-f4** - Develops a piece, controls central squares\n3. **N: b1-c3** - Develops Knight, controls central squares and potentially challenges the Queen\n4. **N: b1-d2** - Defends against the Knight on e4, prepares piece coordination\n\nGiven the precarious position with Black's overwhelming material advantage and my exposed King, the absolute priority is King safety. Castling kingside (K: e1-g1) is the best move because:\n- It removes my King from the dangerous center\n- It connects my Rooks\n- It's a fundamental principle to castle early, especially when behind in material\n- It prepares to potentially create some counterplay with my Rooks\n\nReason: With Black having a huge material advantage and active pieces (especially the centralized Queen on d5 and Knight on e4), my King on e1 is extremely vulnerable. Castling kingside is the most urgent move to get my King to safety and give myself any chance of prolonged resistance. Any development move that leaves my King in the center risks immediate tactical threats.\n\nAnswer: K: e1-g1"}
